From 86badc897aa7d2dfc2dc62d8858f25f159a7f797 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 07:16:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/20] feat(controller): reclaim allocations whose owner no
 longer exists

An allocation outlives its owner only when the resource is removed
without the controlled delete (a direct delete() or data left by an
earlier version). Nothing released such an allocation, so its devices
could never be allocated again.

The controller manager now reclaims these orphans when it starts and
after every periodic resync: for each Active allocation whose owner
(same tenant, same uid) no longer exists, the deployment releaser
cancels that uid's runs and stops their instances, and the allocation
is then marked Expired in a single statement that re-checks the owner,
which frees its devices. A failed stop keeps the allocation for the
next reclaim. The app wires this whenever a launcher is configured.

The reclaim scans for orphans rather than using lease_until. The
controller is the only writer (ADR-013), so there is no lease another
process would have to take over, renewals would only add writes, and
after a long outage it would be the leases of live deployments that
expire, so the owner would still have to be checked before reclaiming.
Allocations of existing resources, Failed ones included, are never
touched by the scan.
